# AGENTS.md

Shared instructions for every AI coding agent working in this repository (Claude Code, Kilo Code, OpenCode, Codex
CLI, GitHub Copilot). Standards and skills are imported from
[agent-standards](https://github.com/Lukk17/agent-standards). The detail behind every rule below is in
[docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md](docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md).

---

### Required opening move

- Before code work, name the skills and subagents that own this task and invoke them, or say none apply and why. This
  is the first line of the reply, on every task, not once per session. A good one names owners and a plan:
  `Owners: markdown-writer for the docs, python-patterns for the test. Delegating to python-pro, code-reviewer after.`
- Delegate investigation, review and bounded implementation by default. Doing a specialist's work inline from the main
  session is the failure this rule exists to prevent.
- Every wiring injects the same reminder into each main-thread prompt and a separate subagent text into each subagent,
  so a subagent does its own task rather than handing it on. Both texts are in
  [docs/GLOBAL_SETUP.md](docs/GLOBAL_SETUP.md).

---

### The preflight gate

[.agents/hooks/preflight_gate.py](.agents/hooks/preflight_gate.py) enforces the opening move on every agent. Before
relying on it or changing its wiring, read
[docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md, The preflight gate](docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md#the-preflight-gate).

- It denies the main thread a write of any file inside the repository, markdown and configuration included, and a
  leading `cd` changes nothing. Delegate the write to a subagent. A write outside the repository, to the null device or
  to `tasks.md` at the root, and switching git branches stay allowed.
- It denies every tool call from a subagent whose definition declares no skills.
- It denies the main thread web fetch and web search on Claude Code. Delegate research to a subagent.
- It denies the main thread running a script, a module, a package or task runner, or a build, unless the command
  passes the gate's built-in list or matches a line of `main-thread-allowlist.txt` at the root. How entries match is in
  [docs/GLOBAL_SETUP.md](docs/GLOBAL_SETUP.md). Ask a subagent to add an entry.
- Every rule fires only on a caller positively identified as the main thread, so GitHub Copilot is ungated. Any error
  inside the gate allows the call, except a path it cannot place, which counts as inside the repository.
- The other hooks in [.agents/hooks/](.agents/hooks/) check reply formatting, lint markdown after an edit and mirror
  the task list into `tasks.md` and `.agents/tasks.widget.json`. Keep both of those files in `.gitignore`.
- Every hook is Python and needs `python3` or `python` on the path. With neither, every hook is silently inert.

---

### Writing AGENTS.md files

- Before changing how these files are written or loaded, read
  [docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md, Instruction files](docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md#instruction-files).
- An AGENTS.md holds only links written as "before X, read Y", and the few rules that no test or guard catches and
  whose breaking is expensive.
- Explanations, reasoning, examples, package or file trees, measured numbers and history go into `docs/` or the
  module's own docs folder, and the AGENTS.md links there.
- A rule a test or guard already enforces is not repeated. Name the guard instead, but only if an agent would otherwise
  not know it exists.
- The root AGENTS.md stays under 200 lines and under 32 KiB, which is the most Codex reads by default, and every other
  AGENTS.md stays under 60 lines.
- Never add a `CLAUDE.md` anywhere in the project, `.claude/CLAUDE.md` and `CLAUDE.local.md` included, because any of
  them switches off native AGENTS.md loading in Claude Code. Your own `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` is outside the project.
- Before adding a line, ask whether an agent would make a mistake without it. If not, it belongs in `docs/`.

---

### Skills

- Skills live only in [.agents/skills/](.agents/skills/). `.claude/skills` is the one symlink to it, so finding no
  skills folder beside your own agent's configuration does not mean the project has none.
- Read the `description` of every skill, open every matching `SKILL.md` in full, and invoke it before the work, for
  example `/code-reviewer` before a review, `/coding-standards` before new code, `/tdd-workflow` before a feature or a
  fix. Run discovery again when the task changes shape.
- Slash commands may show as `/name` or `/name.md` in your autocomplete. Use whichever your agent shows.

---

### Subagents

- The catalogue is [.agents/agents/](.agents/agents/), generated into one tree per agent from `subagents/<name>.md` in
  [agent-standards](https://github.com/Lukk17/agent-standards/tree/master/subagents). Change an agent there, never in
  this project. Before choosing one, read [docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md, Subagents](docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md#subagents).

#### When to use them

- Spawn a subagent whenever one fits, proactively, and in parallel when the work is independent. Tell each one which
  skills to load, for example "review this diff, invoke `/security-review` while you do".
- A review spawns `code-reviewer` plus every specialist the diff touches: `security-auditor` for auth, payments,
  secrets or personal data, `database-expert` for schema or queries, `accessibility-expert` for UI,
  `performance-engineer` after a hot-path edit, `api-tester` for an API change, `legal-advisor` for terms or privacy.
- A feature: `backend-architect` for the contract first, the stack expert for the code, `test-automator` for the tests.
- Debugging: `debugger` for one failing test, `error-detective` across service logs, `devops-troubleshooter` for a
  live incident. Unfamiliar legacy code: `code-archaeologist` first, then `legacy-modernizer`.
- Docs: `docs-architect` for long-form writing, `api-documenter` for API reference.

---

### MCP servers

- Check your tool list at startup and prefer an MCP tool over re-deriving the same answer from local files. The server
  list and the human setup are in [docs/MCP_SETUP.md](docs/MCP_SETUP.md).
- No secret and no substitution token in any MCP configuration. Local servers inherit the environment of the process
  that started the agent.

---

### End-to-end capability tests

- Before adding or running a test under `e2e/testing/`, load the `/e2e-runbooks` skill. During a sweep run one
  `e2e-runner` per spec, within the cap below and the conflict graph each spec's `Concurrency` section defines, and
  never call the API from the main session.
- Replace `<N>` with this project's sweep cap. While it reads `<N>`, the orchestrator asks the user before each sweep.

```text
e2e-runner-max-parallel: <N>
```

---

### Working With Agents

Start every agent from the project root. Every one of them reads this file natively.

- Claude Code: run `claude`. It reads this `AGENTS.md` at session start, and a folder's `AGENTS.md` when it reads a
  file there, because the project has no `CLAUDE.md`. Hooks in [.claude/settings.json](.claude/settings.json), MCP in
  [.mcp.json](.mcp.json).
- Codex: run `codex`. MCP servers and hooks in [.codex/config.toml](.codex/config.toml), agents as TOML in
  `.codex/agents/`.
- OpenCode and Kilo Code: MCP and the hook runner in [opencode.json](opencode.json), agents through the
  `.opencode/agents` and `.kilo/agents` symlinks. Never put `{env:VAR}` in `opencode.json`: Kilo Code rejects the
  whole file and loses every MCP server and the hooks. Run `kilocode config check` after editing it.
- GitHub Copilot: agents in `.github/agents/`, hooks in [.github/hooks/](.github/hooks/), MCP in
  [.vscode/mcp.json](.vscode/mcp.json) for VS Code and [.github/mcp.json](.github/mcp.json) for the CLI. A root
  `.mcp.json` entry of the same name wins over the CLI file.
- On Windows the symlinks need Developer Mode and `git config core.symlinks true`, or each one is checked out as a
  text file and the agent that follows it finds nothing.

---

### Working Principles

- Think before coding. State assumptions, and when the prompt is ambiguous, ask rather than pick a reading silently.
- Simplicity first. Write the minimum that solves the problem, with no unrequested features, abstractions or options.
- Surgical changes. Touch only what the task requires, match the existing style, and remove only what your own change
  orphans.
- Goal-driven execution. Turn the ask into checks, state the plan, and loop until each check passes. Never call a task
  done without running the verification.

---

### OpenSpec Workflow

- When this project uses [OpenSpec](https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec), any change beyond a trivial edit starts
  with `/opsx:propose`. The lifecycle is in [docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md](docs/AGENT_TOOLING.md#openspec-workflow).
- Tick each task in the change's `tasks.md` the moment it is done, before starting the next.

